# Copy to /etc/timberfs/limits.conf.
#
# What this machine will let ONE REQUEST ask for. The ceilings are ON by
# default and this file OVERRIDES them, key by key — a machine nobody
# configured is the one most likely to be asked for everything.
#
# Each value is a number, or `none` to remove that ceiling. `0` is refused:
# a ceiling of zero would answer nothing while reading as "off".
#
# `timberfs limits` prints what is in force and where it came from, and
# exits non-zero on a line it could not use — a line this build cannot use
# is skipped rather than fatal, because `timberfs query` has no startup and
# a refused policy file would answer every caller with a config error they
# cannot fix.
#
# They bound a timberfs-query-document(5) and not the flags beside it: the
# document is the trust boundary and the flags are not — the CLI runs on
# the host, where whoever can type it can already read the files.
#
# A `max` or `deadline` over a ceiling is LOWERED, and the answer is then
# the first page of the one that was asked for — stream-end says which
# ceiling stopped it and the position records resume it. A `tail` over one
# is refused, because a tail answer carries no position to continue from.
#
# The ceilings are declared in every answer that has somewhere to put them,
# so a caller sizes its pages before it asks rather than after an answer
# came back short.
#
# ⚠ This bounds accidents, not adversaries: whoever controls the argv or
# the environment controls these too.

# Most entries one answer may deliver. Default 100000.
#MAX_ENTRIES=100000

# The same in chunks — the unit a `chunks` answer is bounded in, since an
# entry count there caps entries nobody asked about. Default 1000.
#MAX_CHUNKS=1000

# Longest one search may run. It bounds what the other two cannot: a read
# is slow because it READS a lot, not because it matches a lot. Default
# 30000.
#DEADLINE_MS=30000
